// A REFERENCE PARAMETER TAKES THE ADDRESS OF ITS ARGUMENT, so the argument must have
// storage. A call's RESULT does not.
//
// MEASURED before this refusal existed, and it PRE-DATES the receiver-lowering round
// that now owns the site: this file passed the whole front end — lexer, parser, type
// checker, borrow checker, effects — and then died inside gcc with
//   error: cannot take the address of an rvalue of type 'struct C'
// because code generation had emitted `__pd_sink(&(__pd_make()))`. Front-end approval
// plus a C-compiler rejection is the one class this compiler does not ship: it states a
// rule of this language in a compiler that has never heard of it.
//
// NOT REPAIRED BY MATERIALISING A TEMPORARY. The argument-hoisting path emits
// `T* t = &(expr);`, which takes the address of the same rvalue one line earlier, and a
// one-argument call is never hoisted at all. `T t = expr; sink(&t);` would be new
// lowering rather than something that falls out of what is there, so this is refused by
// name and the programmer writes the local — which is also the only spelling that says
// how long the referent lives.
struct C {
    n: i64,
}

fn make() -> C {
    return C { n: 7 };
}

fn sink(c: &C) -> i64 {
    return c.n;
}

fn main() {
    print_int(sink(make()));
}
